window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"title":"Home","url":"","text":"The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget is led by Philippe Ciais and Piyu Ke at the Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE) in France, with the support of research groups from 17 institutions in 8 countries. Since its first edition for 2023, and as part of the Global Carbon Project, the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget has provided early estimates of how much fossil CO₂ stays in the atmosphere and how much is taken up by the ocean and the land. It follows the structure of the annual Global Carbon Budget. Land sink rebound slowed the rise of atmospheric CO₂ in 2025 The global net land sink rebounded to 2.36 ± 0.16 GtC yr⁻¹ in 2025 and the CO₂ growth rate fell by 45% from the 2024 record, according to preliminary results from the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget. The paper is in review. View Budget 2025 Photo: Adriana Brandao (Adrisub), CC BY-SA 4.0 Key Messages from Budget 2025 View Image: NASA/JPL-Caltech Read the July 2024–June 2025 Budget in Full Advanced Science website link Photo: Gpgeo, CC BY-SA 4.0 Late-2024 carbon losses dominated the July 2024–June 2025 budget JULY 2024–JUNE 2025 View Photo: Brian Ralphs, CC BY 2.0 Record CO₂ growth as land regions turned from sinks to sources JANUARY–DECEMBER 2024 View Photo: Imam4rake, CC BY-SA 4.0 El Niño drove the highest July-to-July CO₂ growth since 1979 JULY 2023–JUNE 2024 View Photo: The Interior, CC BY-SA 4.0 Land carbon sink fell to its weakest level since 2003 JANUARY–DECEMBER 2023 View Photo: Diego Delso, delso.photo, CC BY-SA 4.0 FAQs about the Low-latency Budget View FAQs Photo: Wesley Sousa/MCTI, CC BY 2.0 About the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget LEARN MORE ABOUT HOW THE BUDGET IS MADE Read Budget 2025 in figures and graphs View A closer look at the data The Latest Low-latency Data (2025)"},{"title":"Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2023","url":"editions/2023/","text":"The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2023 was the first low-latency budget. It covers January to December 2023 and was published in National Science Review on 22 October 2024. In 2023 the CO₂ growth rate reached a record 3.37 ± 0.11 ppm yr⁻¹ at Mauna Loa (2.82 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ for the marine boundary layer), 86% above 2022, while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.1–1.1%. Vegetation models and an OCO-2 inversion place the global net land sink at 0.44 ± 0.21 GtC yr⁻¹, the weakest since 2003. The largest abnormal losses were in the Amazon during the drought of the second half of 2023, and from extreme fires in Canada and Southeast Asia. Key messages View the key messages Publications National Science Review (open access) Full text and supplementary information (PMC) arXiv:2407.12447 Press releases EurekAlert! Figures Figures in the paper and supplementary information FAQs View FAQs Data The Data Hub Archive of previous budgets Datasets Key numbers as published [csv] View the 2025 budget here View the Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget here View the 2024 budget here View the Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget here"},{"title":"Key Messages from Budget 2023","url":"editions/2023/key-messages/","text":"The main findings for 2023, as published in National Science Review. The global land carbon sink fell to its weakest level since 2003 The first low-latency budget. In 2023 the CO₂ growth rate hit a record high while fossil emissions barely grew, implying an unprecedented weakening of natural sinks. Bottom-up models and an OCO-2 inversion place the global net land sink at 0.44 ± 0.21 GtC yr⁻¹, driven by the Amazon drought, extreme fires in Canada and heat across the northern mid-latitudes. The CO₂ growth rate reached a record 3.37 ± 0.11 ppm yr⁻¹ at Mauna Loa (2.82 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ for the marine boundary layer), 86% above 2022, while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.1–1.1%. The global net land sink fell to 0.44 ± 0.21 GtC yr⁻¹, the weakest since 2003. The largest abnormal losses were in the Amazon during the drought of the second half of 2023 (0.31 ± 0.19 GtC yr⁻¹), from extreme fires in Canada (0.58 ± 0.10 GtC yr⁻¹) and in Southeast Asia (0.13 ± 0.12 GtC yr⁻¹). Since 2015 the land sink north of 20°N has declined by half, to 1.13 ± 0.24 GtC yr⁻¹ in 2023, while the tropics switched to a carbon loss (0.56 ± 0.23 GtC yr⁻¹) with the developing El Niño. Land regions exposed to extreme heat contributed a gross carbon loss of 1.73 GtC yr⁻¹. The budget at a glance Quantity January–December 2023 Notes Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate 2.82 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ NOAA marine boundary layer; 3.37 ± 0.11 at Mauna Loa (record high) Fossil CO₂ emissions 10.20 ± 0.05 GtC yr⁻¹ +0.1% to +1.1% compared with 2022 Net land sink 0.44 ± 0.21 GtC yr⁻¹ Mean of DGVMs (0.14 ± 0.28) and OCO-2 inversion (0.73 ± 0.30) Ocean sink 2.60 ± 0.72 GtC yr⁻¹ Ocean model emulators; OCO-2 inversion 2.33 ± 0.20 Budget imbalance 1.26 GtC yr⁻¹ Against the marine boundary layer growth rate; 0.09 against Mauna Loa Values as published. Uncertainties are ±1σ across ensemble members unless stated. Models and data used Fossil CO₂ emissions. Carbon Monitor and the Global Carbon Budget method (average of the two) Atmospheric growth rate. NOAA marine boundary layer (MBL) and Mauna Loa (MLO) Land, bottom-up. 3 DGVMs driven by ERA5: ORCHIDEE, JULES, OCN; fire emissions from GFED4.1s and GFAS Ocean, bottom-up. Machine-learning emulators of 5 ocean biogeochemical models and 8 data products (GCB 2022) Top-down. 1 OCO-2 atmospheric inversion: CAMS Anomaly reference. 2015–2022 Publication Ke P, Ciais P, Sitch S, Li W, Bastos A, Liu Z, et al. Low latency carbon budget analysis reveals a large decline of the land carbon sink in 2023. National Science Review , Natl Sci Rev 2024; 11(12): nwae367. https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwae367 Back to the 2023 budget"},{"title":"Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, July 2023–June 2024","url":"editions/2023-24/","text":"This edition covers July 2023 to June 2024 and follows the carbon cycle through the decay phase of the 2023/24 El Niño. It was the first July–June edition and was published in iScience on 10 August 2026. Atmospheric CO₂ grew by 3.66 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹, the highest July-to-July growth rate since 1979. The growth-rate anomaly was mainly driven by a 2.24 GtC yr⁻¹ weakening of the net land sink, partly offset by 0.38 GtC yr⁻¹ stronger ocean uptake. The tropics accounted for 97.5% of the land anomaly, led by the Amazon, central Africa and Southeast Asia. The weakening of the land sink persisted through the first half of 2024, beyond the late-2023 Amazon drought. Key messages View the key messages Publications iScience (open access) Full text and supplementary information (PMC) arXiv:2504.09189 Figures Figures in the paper and supplementary information FAQs View FAQs Data The Data Hub Archive of previous budgets Datasets Key numbers as published [csv] View the 2025 budget here View the Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget here View the 2024 budget here View the 2023 budget here"},{"title":"Key Messages from Budget 2023–24","url":"editions/2023-24/key-messages/","text":"The main findings for July 2023 to June 2024, as published in iScience. The 2023/24 El Niño drove a record CO₂ growth rate through a weaker tropical land sink Extending the budget through the decay phase of the 2023/24 El Niño, this edition finds the highest July-to-June CO₂ growth rate since 1979. The anomaly was driven by a 2.24 GtC yr⁻¹ weakening of the net land sink, 97.5% of it in the tropics, partly offset by stronger ocean uptake. From July 2023 to June 2024 atmospheric CO₂ grew by 3.66 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹, the highest July-to-July rate since 1979; after detrending, the anomaly (1.1 ppm yr⁻¹) was comparable to the 1997/98 and 2015/16 El Niño events. The anomaly was mainly driven by a 2.24 GtC yr⁻¹ weakening of the net land sink, partly offset by 0.38 GtC yr⁻¹ stronger ocean uptake. The tropics accounted for 97.5% of the land anomaly, led by the Amazon, central Africa and Southeast Asia. The land-sink weakening persisted through the first half of 2024, beyond the late-2023 Amazon drought, with new source anomalies in tropical Africa and Southeast Asia. ERA5-driven DGVMs give larger tropical losses than the OCO-2 inversions; emulators of 19 GCB DGVMs and the SiB4 model sit closer to the inversions. The budget at a glance Quantity July 2023–June 2024 Notes Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate 3.66 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ NOAA marine boundary layer, July–June; highest since 1979 Fossil CO₂ emissions 10.14 GtC yr⁻¹ Range 10.14–10.15 across two estimates Net land flux −0.34 ± 0.33 GtC yr⁻¹ A net source. DGVMs −0.59 ± 0.60; inversions −0.09 ± 0.26 (negative = source) Ocean sink 2.56 ± 0.33 GtC yr⁻¹ Emulators 2.87 ± 0.56; inversions 2.25 ± 0.33 Budget imbalance 0.09 GtC yr⁻¹ Bottom-up budget, against the marine boundary layer growth rate Values as published, for July 2023–June 2024. Uncertainties are ±1σ across ensemble members. Models and data used Fossil CO₂ emissions. Carbon Monitor and the Global Carbon Budget method; monthly split from IEA-EDGAR Atmospheric growth rate. NOAA MBL (June–July); OCO-2 GRESO and inversion-implied growth as cross-checks Land, bottom-up. 4 DGVMs driven by ERA5: JULES, OCN, ORCHIDEE-MICT, LPJ-EOSIM; cross-checks from SiB4 (CTE-HR) and emulators of 19 GCB 2024 DGVMs Ocean, bottom-up. Machine-learning emulators of GCB 2022 ocean models and data products Top-down. 3 OCO-2 inversions: CAMS, CMS-Flux, GONGGA Anomaly reference. 2015–2022, same calendar window Publication Ke P, Ciais P, Yao Y, Sitch S, Li W, Xu Y, et al. Low latency global carbon budget reveals land sink decline during 2023 to 2024 El Niño. iScience , iScience 2026; 29(8): 117053. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.117053 Back to the Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget"},{"title":"Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2024","url":"editions/2024/","text":"The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2024 covers January to December 2024 and was published in National Science Review on 15 January 2026. The CO₂ growth rate from the NOAA marine boundary layer network reached a record 3.73 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹, a 38% increase over 2023, while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.85%. The vegetation models estimate a net land source of 0.65 ± 0.85 GtC yr⁻¹, the weakest net land flux since 1987, and the OCO-2 inversions give a small sink of 0.29 ± 0.80 GtC yr⁻¹. Tropical lands were a net source, with the largest anomalies in the Amazon and central Africa. Fire emissions were 0.50 GtC yr⁻¹ above the 2015–2022 mean. Key messages View the key messages Publications National Science Review (open access) Full text and supplementary information (PMC) Figures Figures in the paper and supplementary information FAQs View FAQs Data The Data Hub Archive of previous budgets Datasets Key numbers as published [csv] View the 2025 budget here View the Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget here View the Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget here View the 2023 budget here"},{"title":"Key Messages from Budget 2024","url":"editions/2024/key-messages/","text":"The main findings for 2024, as published in National Science Review. Record CO₂ growth in 2024 as land regions turned from sinks to sources In 2024 the marine-boundary-layer CO₂ growth rate reached a record 3.73 ppm yr⁻¹ while fossil emissions grew by only 0.85%. Bottom-up models and OCO-2 inversions both show a strong weakening of the global net land sink and widespread transitions of terrestrial regions from sinks to sources. The CO₂ growth rate from the NOAA marine boundary layer network reached a record 3.73 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹, a 38% increase over 2023, while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.85%. The DGVMs estimate a net land source of 0.65 ± 0.85 GtC yr⁻¹, the weakest net land flux since 1987; the OCO-2 inversions give a small sink of 0.29 ± 0.80 GtC yr⁻¹. Tropical lands were a net source of 1.26 GtC yr⁻¹ (DGVMs) and 0.95 GtC yr⁻¹ (inversions); the Amazon (drought and fires) and central Africa had the largest source anomalies. The northern land sink (north of 30°N) continued its decline since 2015, to 0.90 GtC yr⁻¹. Fire emissions were 0.50 GtC yr⁻¹ above the 2015–2022 mean, about 20% of the land-sink reduction. The budget at a glance Quantity January–December 2024 Notes Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate 3.73 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ NOAA marine boundary layer; record high since 1959 Fossil CO₂ emissions 10.19 ± 0.01 GtC yr⁻¹ +0.85% compared with 2023 Net land flux −0.65 ± 0.85 GtC yr⁻¹ DGVMs: a net source, the weakest since 1987. OCO-2 inversions: a sink of 0.29 ± 0.80 Ocean sink 2.48 ± 0.29 GtC yr⁻¹ Emulators 2.66 ± 0.55; inversions 2.30 ± 0.19 Budget imbalance 0.26 ± 1.03 GtC yr⁻¹ Bottom-up budget, against the marine boundary layer growth rate Values as published. Uncertainties are ±1σ across ensemble members. The ocean sink excludes the river-flux adjustment used in the Global Carbon Budget. Models and data used Fossil CO₂ emissions. Carbon Monitor and Global Carbon Budget 2024 projections Atmospheric growth rate. NOAA MBL; OCO-2 GRESO and inversion-implied growth as cross-checks Land, bottom-up. 4 DGVMs driven by ERA5: JULES, OCN, ORCHIDEE-MICT, LPJ-EOSIM; SiB4 (CTE-HR) as a cross-check Ocean, bottom-up. Machine-learning emulators of ocean models and data products Top-down. 3 OCO-2 inversions: CAMS, CMS-Flux, GONGGA Anomaly reference. 2015–2022 Publication Ciais P, Ke P, Yao Y, Sitch S, Li W, Xu Y, et al. Low latency global carbon budget indicates reduced land carbon sink in the year 2024. National Science Review , Natl Sci Rev 2026; 13(2): nwaf594. https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwaf594 Back to the 2024 budget"},{"title":"Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, July 2024–June 2025","url":"editions/2024-25/","text":"This edition covers July 2024 to June 2025, the year after the 2023/24 El Niño. It was published online in Advanced Science on 1 September 2026. The CO₂ growth rate was 2.62 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹, down from 3.66 ppm yr⁻¹ during the El Niño but 6.5% above the 2013–2022 July-to-June mean. Combining bottom-up and top-down estimates, the net land sink was 1.06 ± 0.40 GtC yr⁻¹. This was stronger than in 2023/24 but 0.49 GtC yr⁻¹ weaker than the 2015–2022 mean, mostly because of losses in late 2024. Whether the land had recovered by early 2025 depends on the method. Ocean uptake shows no global weakening. Key messages View the key messages Publications Advanced Science (open access) Full text and supplementary information (PMC) Figures Figures in the paper and supplementary information FAQs View FAQs Data The Data Hub Archive of previous budgets Datasets Key numbers as published [csv] View the 2025 budget here View the 2024 budget here View the Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget here View the 2023 budget here"},{"title":"Key Messages from Budget 2024–25","url":"editions/2024-25/key-messages/","text":"The main findings for July 2024 to June 2025, as published in Advanced Science. Late-2024 carbon losses dominated the year; early-2025 land recovery signals differ between methods After the 2023/24 El Niño, the CO₂ growth rate fell but stayed above average. The land sink strengthened relative to the El Niño year yet remained weaker than the 2015–2022 mean, mostly because of losses in late 2024. Whether the land had recovered by early 2025 depends on the method: models retain a deficit, while all inversions show fluxes close to normal. The CO₂ growth rate was 2.62 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ from July 2024 to June 2025, down from 3.66 ppm yr⁻¹ during the El Niño but 6.5% above the 2013–2022 July-to-June mean. Combining bottom-up and top-down estimates, the net land sink was 1.06 ± 0.40 GtC yr⁻¹: stronger than in 2023/24, but 0.49 GtC yr⁻¹ weaker than the 2015–2022 mean. The annual land anomaly was dominated by late-2024 losses: in the DGVMs, July–December 2024 accounted for 86% of the annual deficit. Early-2025 recovery is method-dependent: DGVMs retain a weak deficit, whereas all inversions indicate fluxes close to the reference mean and a stronger-than-normal northern sink in late spring 2025. Ocean uptake shows no global weakening. Temperature explains most of the global land-flux variability, while terrestrial water storage matters more regionally. The budget at a glance Quantity July 2024–June 2025 Notes Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate 2.62 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ NOAA marine boundary layer, July–June; 6.5% above the 2013–2022 mean Fossil CO₂ emissions 10.35 GtC yr⁻¹ Range 10.33–10.37 across two estimates Net land sink 1.06 ± 0.40 GtC yr⁻¹ Mean of DGVMs (0.55 ± 0.31) and inversions (1.58 ± 0.73); 0.49 below the 2015–2022 mean Ocean sink 3.06 ± 0.34 GtC yr⁻¹ Emulators 3.19 ± 0.65; inversions 2.93 ± 0.17 Budget imbalance 1.05 ± 0.70 GtC yr⁻¹ 95% interval −0.44 to 2.23, not significantly different from zero Values as published, for July 2024–June 2025. Uncertainties are ±1σ; the budget imbalance uncertainty comes from Monte Carlo propagation. Models and data used Fossil CO₂ emissions. Carbon Monitor and the Global Carbon Budget method; monthly split from IEA-EDGAR Atmospheric growth rate. NOAA MBL (June–July); OCO-2 and GRESO growth rates in a sensitivity test Land, bottom-up. 3 DGVMs driven by ERA5: JULES, ORCHIDEE, LPJ-EOSIM; emulators of 20 GCB 2025 DGVMs as a benchmark Ocean, bottom-up. Machine-learning emulators of 10 ocean models and 9 data products (GCB 2025) Top-down. 4 OCO-2 inversions: CAMS, CMS-Flux, GONGGA, CTE Anomaly reference. 2015–2022, July–June windows Publication Ke P, Ciais P, Yao Y, Sitch S, Li W, Du X, et al. Low Latency Global Carbon Budget Reveals Late 2024 Carbon Losses and Contrasting Early 2025 Land Sink Recovery Signals. Advanced Science , Adv Sci 2026: e77369. https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.77369 Back to the Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget"},{"title":"Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2025","url":"editions/2025/","text":"These are preliminary results. The paper describing this budget is in review. The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2025 covers January to December 2025. The paper is in review and the values on this page are preliminary. The CO₂ growth rate from the NOAA marine boundary layer network fell by 45%, from a record 3.76 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ in 2024 (3.73 in the 2024 edition) to 2.06 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ in 2025, even as fossil CO₂ emissions rose by 0.7% to 10.38 GtC yr⁻¹. The global net land sink rebounded to 2.36 ± 0.16 GtC yr⁻¹, a strengthening of 2.81 ± 0.31 GtC yr⁻¹ from 2024. The ocean sink was similar to 2024, at 3.11 ± 0.36 GtC yr⁻¹. The recovery was strongest in the tropics and incomplete across the northern extratropics. Key messages View the key messages Publications Low latency global carbon budget reveals strong land sink recovery in 2025 (manuscript in review) Figures Budget 2025 in figures and graphs FAQs View FAQs Data The Data Hub Archive of previous budgets Datasets Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2025 v1.0 [xlsx] Global budget time series [csv] Gridded land flux anomalies, losses and recovery (1°) [netCDF] Key numbers (preliminary) [csv] View the Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget here View the 2024 budget here View the Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget here View the 2023 budget here"},{"title":"Key Messages from Budget 2025","url":"editions/2025/key-messages/","text":"These are preliminary results. The paper describing this budget is in review. The main findings for 2025, from the manuscript in review. All values are preliminary. A strong rebound of the land sink slowed the rise of atmospheric CO₂ in 2025 The CO₂ growth rate fell by 45% from the 2024 record, even though fossil emissions rose to a new high. Bottom-up models and four OCO-2 inversions agree that the land sink rebounded strongly after the 2023–2024 weakening, while the ocean sink changed little. The recovery was strongest in the tropics and incomplete across the northern extratropics. The CO₂ growth rate from the NOAA marine boundary layer network fell by 45%, from a record 3.76 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ in 2024 to 2.06 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ in 2025, below the 2015–2022 mean of 2.47 ppm yr⁻¹, even as fossil CO₂ emissions rose by 0.7% to 10.38 GtC yr⁻¹. The global net land sink rebounded to 2.36 ± 0.16 GtC yr⁻¹, a strengthening of 2.81 ± 0.31 GtC yr⁻¹ from 2024, and 0.71 ± 0.13 GtC yr⁻¹ above the 2015–2022 mean. The ocean sink (3.11 ± 0.36 GtC yr⁻¹) was similar to 2024: the rebound of the land sink was the dominant change behind the drop in CO₂ growth. Where the land sink had weakened substantially in 2023–2024, about 80% of the land area showed some recovery in 2025; carbon-weighted recovery reached 87.3% (DGVMs) and 99.5% (inversions) of the earlier loss. Recovery exceeded 100% in the tropics (106.4% DGVMs, 116.4% inversions) but remained incomplete in the northern extratropics (59.6% and 90.7%). The budget at a glance Quantity January–December 2025 Notes Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate 2.06 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹ NOAA marine boundary layer; 3.76 ± 0.09 in 2024 (−45%) Fossil CO₂ emissions 10.38 GtC yr⁻¹ +0.7% compared with 2024 Net land sink 2.36 ± 0.16 GtC yr⁻¹ Mean of DGVMs (2.04 ± 0.24) and inversions (2.68 ± 0.20); +2.81 ± 0.31 compared with 2024 Ocean sink 3.11 ± 0.36 GtC yr⁻¹ Emulators 3.15 ± 0.65; inversions 3.06 ± 0.32 Budget imbalance 0.81 ± 0.69 GtC yr⁻¹ 95% interval −0.66 to 1.91, not significantly different from zero Preliminary values from the manuscript in review. Uncertainties are ±1σ; changes and anomalies use member-wise paired differences; the imbalance uncertainty comes from Monte Carlo propagation. Models and data used Fossil CO₂ emissions. Carbon Monitor and Global Carbon Budget 2025 projections (range 10.34–10.42 GtC yr⁻¹) Atmospheric growth rate. NOAA MBL; GRESO (OCO-2 and GOSAT) and inversion-implied growth as cross-checks Land, bottom-up. 3 DGVMs driven by ERA5: JULES, ORCHIDEE-MICT, LPJ-EOSIM; fire emissions from GFED4.1s and GFAS Ocean, bottom-up. Machine-learning emulators of 10 ocean models and 9 data products (GCB 2025) Top-down. 4 OCO-2 inversions: CAMS, CMS-Flux, CTE, GONGGA, with a riverine transport correction Anomaly reference. 2015–2022 Publication Ciais P, Ke P, Tian X, Sitch S, Li W, Du X, et al. Low latency global carbon budget reveals strong land sink recovery in 2025. Manuscript in review. Back to the 2025 budget"},{"title":"Budget 2025 in figures and graphs","url":"editions/2025/figures/","text":"These are preliminary results. The paper describing this budget is in review. Figures and graphs for 2025. The values are preliminary until the paper is published. The global carbon budget, 2010–2025 Billion tonnes of carbon per year (GtC yr⁻¹). Sources above zero; where the carbon went below zero. Bottom-up (models) Top-down (OCO-2 inversions) Show table Download data The bottom-up budget combines fossil emissions, the NOAA marine boundary layer growth rate, ocean model emulators and dynamic global vegetation models. The budget imbalance is the part the models do not explain. The top-down budget uses the mean of the OCO-2 inversions from 2015. Point at a year to see the values. Atmospheric CO₂ growth rate Parts per million per year (ppm yr⁻¹). 1 ppm = 2.124 GtC. Show table Download data Bars show the NOAA globally averaged marine boundary layer record (±1σ). Markers show growth rates derived from OCO-2 satellite data (GRESO) and implied by the OCO-2 inversions. Net land sink by method GtC yr⁻¹. Uptake is positive. Shading shows the spread across models (±1σ). Show table Download data Ocean sink by method GtC yr⁻¹. Uptake is positive. Shading shows the spread across models (±1σ). Show table Download data CO₂ growth, El Niño and temperature, month by month Three panels on a shared time axis. Show table Download data Top: monthly global CO₂ growth rate from satellite observations (GRESO). Middle: Oceanic Niño Index (ONI); values above +0.5 °C indicate El Niño and below −0.5 °C La Niña. Bottom: global surface temperature anomaly (GISTEMP). Sinks are shown as positive values: a negative net land sink means the land released CO₂. Shading shows the spread across models or inversions (±1σ). Top-down estimates start in 2015, when the OCO-2 inversions begin. Back to the 2025 budget"},{"title":"About us","url":"about/","text":"The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget is an activity of the Global Carbon Project. It provides an early estimate of the global CO₂ budget, within months of the end of each period and ahead of the annual Global Carbon Budget. Each edition estimates fossil CO₂ emissions, the growth of CO₂ in the atmosphere and the uptake by the ocean and the land. The first low-latency budget covered 2023. That year the CO₂ growth rate hit a record high while fossil emissions barely grew, which implied a large decline of the land carbon sink. The low-latency budget gives an early signal of such changes in the land and ocean sinks. The 2023 budget was published in National Science Review in October 2024. Three more editions have since been published, for July 2023 to June 2024 (iScience), for 2024 (National Science Review) and for July 2024 to June 2025 (Advanced Science). The 2025 edition is in review. The activity is led by Philippe Ciais and Piyu Ke at the Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE) in France. Each edition is a collaboration of land, ocean and atmospheric inversion modelling groups. Each paper and its authors are cited on the Key Messages page of that edition. The annual Global Carbon Budget is the reference assessment. It is published every year with complete data up to the previous year and uses large model ensembles. The low-latency budget follows the same structure but uses fewer models, chosen for speed and calibrated against the Global Carbon Budget ensemble. Its numbers are preliminary and are superseded by the Global Carbon Budget when that becomes available. Two editions are prepared each year. The calendar-year edition covers January to December and is prepared in the first months of the following year. The July–June edition spans the typical El Niño and La Niña cycle and is prepared in the second half of the year. Low-latency Global Carbon Budget Leads Philippe Ciais Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE), France philippe.ciais@cea.fr Piyu Ke Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE), France piyu.ke@lsce.ipsl.fr Contributing Institutions Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE/IPSL), France University of Exeter, United Kingdom Tsinghua University, China Institute of Tibetan Plateau Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences, China Peking University, China Wageningen University, the Netherlands NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory, USA Colorado State University, USA NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory, USA Spark Climate Solutions, USA Microsoft Research, China Columbia University, USA Leipzig University, Germany Max Planck Institute for Biogeochemistry, Germany CICERO Center for International Climate Research, Norway The University of Hong Kong, China Seoul National University, Republic of Korea How the budget is made The budget follows the structure of the Global Carbon Budget. Fossil CO₂ emissions are either added to the atmosphere or taken up by the ocean and the land. Every term is estimated in two independent ways. The bottom-up budget uses process models and data-driven products. The top-down budget uses atmospheric inversions of OCO-2 satellite observations. Fossil CO₂ emissions are the average of two near-real-time estimates. Carbon Monitor is based on daily activity data in six sectors. The Global Carbon Budget method is based on preliminary energy statistics and projections. The growth of CO₂ in the atmosphere comes from the NOAA globally averaged marine boundary layer record (the 2023 edition also reports the Mauna Loa record), with growth rates from OCO-2 and from the inversions as cross-checks. The land sink is estimated with dynamic global vegetation models driven by ERA5 weather. Their mean level is calibrated to the median of the Global Carbon Budget 2023 models over 2019–2022, so the low-latency models supply the year-to-year anomalies. Fire emissions in the models are replaced by the mean of the GFED4.1s and GFAS satellite p"},{"title":"The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget FAQs","url":"faq/","text":"What is the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget? The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget is an early, twice-yearly assessment of the global CO₂ budget. It estimates how much CO₂ is emitted by fossil fuels and cement, how much stays in the atmosphere, and how much is taken up by the ocean and the land. It combines near-real-time fossil emission estimates, dynamic global vegetation models (DGVMs) driven by ERA5 weather data, machine-learning emulators of ocean models and data products, and atmospheric inversions constrained by the OCO-2 satellite. It is an activity of the Global Carbon Project. How does it relate to the annual Global Carbon Budget? The Global Carbon Budget (GCB) is the reference assessment. It is published every year with complete data up to the previous year and uses large model ensembles. The low-latency budget covers the most recent months. It uses fewer models, chosen for speed and calibrated against the GCB ensemble, and gives an early signal of changes in the land and ocean sinks. Its numbers are preliminary and are superseded by the GCB when that becomes available. How is it produced so quickly? Each component uses inputs that are available within weeks. Fossil emissions come from daily Carbon Monitor estimates, ERA5 reanalysis weather drives the DGVMs, fire emissions come from GFED4.1s and GFAS, and OCO-2 retrievals are released about six weeks after measurement. Machine-learning emulators extend the ocean models and data products to the latest month. Why do the vegetation models and the atmospheric inversions give different numbers? The two methods are independent. DGVMs simulate land fluxes from weather and CO₂ and never see atmospheric CO₂ measurements. They can over-react to drought, for example because they lack groundwater access, drought-induced tree mortality and the slow decay of deadwood. Inversions are fitted to satellite CO₂ observations, but they depend on atmospheric transport, prior fluxes and where the satellite can see. We report both, and their mean, so that agreement and disagreement between them are visible. What is the budget imbalance? In the bottom-up budget, the imbalance is fossil emissions minus the atmospheric CO₂ increase and the land and ocean sinks estimated by models. A positive imbalance means that the models underestimate the sinks, or that emissions are overestimated. The top-down budget closes almost by construction, because the inversions are fitted to atmospheric observations. Since the July 2024–June 2025 edition, the imbalance is given with a 95% interval from Monte Carlo propagation. Why are some editions July–June instead of calendar years? El Niño and La Niña events usually develop in the second half of one year and decay in the first half of the next. A July–June window captures the carbon-cycle response to such an event in one period. It also allows an update in the middle of the year. Why is 2015–2022 used as the reference period? It is the common period before 2023 that is covered by all the satellite-based inversions and the models. Using the same years for every method means that anomalies can be compared like for like. How are the uncertainties calculated? Values given as mean ± 1σ are the ensemble mean and the standard deviation across the models or inversions. This is a measure of spread, not a full uncertainty. Changes between years, and anomalies relative to 2015–2022, are calculated member by member before averaging, so that the persistent biases of each model cancel. The combined land or ocean sink is the mean of the bottom-up and top-down estimates. Its uncertainty is half the root-sum-square of their two standard deviations. What is the net land sink? The net land sink is the net CO₂ exchange between the land and the atmosphere. It includes photosynthesis, respiration, fires, rivers and land-use change emissions. In Global Carbon Budget terms it is close to the natural land sink minus land-use change emissions. On this website a positive value is uptake "},{"title":"The Data Hub","url":"data/","text":"Welcome Since its first edition for 2023, the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget has provided early estimates of fossil CO₂ emissions, the atmospheric CO₂ growth rate and the land and ocean sinks. Here you can access the budget data from the latest edition and the key numbers from every edition. For access to the summary data featuring in the latest edition, visit the Latest Data page. The key numbers from previous editions, as published, are available in the Archive . All data are free to use under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0). Please read the Fair Use Statements before using them."},{"title":"The Latest Low-latency Data (2025)","url":"data/latest/","text":"These data belong to a budget whose paper is still in review. They appear in this preview only. Below you can find the datasets featured in the latest edition of the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget. The Excel workbook has a README sheet that describes its contents. Please refer to the Fair Use Statements below before using the datasets. Data from an edition whose paper is still in review are preliminary and may change. Sinks are positive when carbon is taken up from the atmosphere. Fluxes are in GtC yr⁻¹ (1 GtC = 3.664 Gt CO₂) and atmospheric growth is in ppm yr⁻¹ (1 ppm = 2.124 GtC). Anomalies are relative to the 2015–2022 mean of the same calendar window. Latest datasets Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2025 v1.0 [xlsx] (All tables with a README sheet) Global budget time series [csv] (Bottom-up, top-down and combined, with uncertainties) Gridded land flux anomalies, losses and recovery (1°) [netCDF] (DGVM and inversion ensemble means; 2015–2022 reference; CF metadata) Key numbers (preliminary) [csv] (Headline values from the manuscript in review) Fair Use Statements The data provided on this site are freely available under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC BY 4.0). Citation: Please cite the paper of the edition you use. The papers are listed on each edition page and in the Archive. Numbers for the same year can differ between editions. Acknowledgement: Please add the following text in the acknowledgement of your paper: “We acknowledge the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, an activity of the Global Carbon Project, and we thank the land, ocean and atmospheric inversion modelling groups for producing and making available their output.” Individual model output: For output of individual models, please contact the leads of this activity, who will put you in touch with the modelling groups. Contact: For questions about the data, please contact Philippe Ciais ( philippe.ciais@cea.fr ) or Piyu Ke ( piyu.ke@lsce.ipsl.fr ) at LSCE."},{"title":"Archive","url":"archive/","text":"Take a look back through previous editions of the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, from the first budget for 2023 onwards. Numbers for the same year can differ between editions. The latest edition is the current best estimate, including for earlier years. January–December 2025 (in review) The 2025 budget Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2025 v1.0 [xlsx] Global budget time series [csv] Gridded land flux anomalies, losses and recovery (1°) [netCDF] Key numbers (preliminary) [csv] July 2024–June 2025 The Jul 2024–Jun 2025 budget Key numbers as published [csv] Advanced Science (open access) [website] Full text and supplementary information (PMC) [website] January–December 2024 The 2024 budget Key numbers as published [csv] National Science Review (open access) [website] Full text and supplementary information (PMC) [website] July 2023–June 2024 The Jul 2023–Jun 2024 budget Key numbers as published [csv] iScience (open access) [website] Full text and supplementary information (PMC) [website] January–December 2023 The 2023 budget Key numbers as published [csv] National Science Review (open access) [website] Full text and supplementary information (PMC) [website]"},{"title":"News","url":"news/","text":"News from the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, including new editions and publications. Photo: Gpgeo, CC BY-SA 4.0 1 September 2026 Late-2024 carbon losses dominated the July 2024–June 2025 budget The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget for July 2024 to June 2025 was published online in Advanced Science. The net land sink was 1.06 ± 0.40 GtC yr⁻¹, stronger than during the 2023/24 El Niño but 0.49 GtC yr⁻¹ weaker than the 2015–2022 mean. Models and inversions differ on whether the land had recovered by early 2025. Read Photo: Imam4rake, CC BY-SA 4.0 10 August 2026 El Niño weakened the tropical land sink from July 2023 to June 2024 The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget for July 2023 to June 2024 was published in iScience. Atmospheric CO₂ grew by 3.66 ± 0.09 ppm yr⁻¹, the highest July-to-July growth rate since 1979. The growth-rate anomaly was mainly driven by a 2.24 GtC yr⁻¹ weakening of the net land sink, 97.5% of it in the tropics. Read Photo: Brian Ralphs, CC BY 2.0 15 January 2026 Record CO₂ growth in 2024 as the land sink weakened The Low-latency Global Carbon Budget 2024 was published in National Science Review. The CO₂ growth rate reached a record 3.73 ± 0.08 ppm yr⁻¹ while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.85%. Vegetation models and OCO-2 inversions both show a strong weakening of the global net land sink. Read Photo: The Interior, CC BY-SA 4.0 22 October 2024 Land carbon sink in 2023 weakest since 2003 The first Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, for 2023, was published in National Science Review. The global net land sink fell to 0.44 ± 0.21 GtC yr⁻¹, the weakest since 2003, while fossil CO₂ emissions increased by only 0.1–1.1%. The largest abnormal losses came from the Amazon drought and from extreme fires in Canada. Read"},{"title":"Contact us","url":"contact/","text":"For more information about the Low-latency Global Carbon Budget, or about use of the data, please contact Philippe Ciais ( philippe.ciais@cea.fr ) or Piyu Ke ( piyu.ke@lsce.ipsl.fr ) at the Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE), France. For output of individual models, please contact the leads, who will put you in touch with the modelling groups. For the annual Global Carbon Budget, please visit globalcarbonbudget.org . Philippe Ciais philippe.ciais@cea.fr Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE), France Piyu Ke piyu.ke@lsce.ipsl.fr Laboratoire des Sciences du Climat et de l'Environnement (LSCE), France"},{"title":"Photo credits","url":"credits/","text":"The photographs on this website are used under the licences listed below. Images released under a Creative Commons ShareAlike licence have been cropped and resized for this website; the modified versions are shared under the same licence. Earth Observation taken by the Expedition 20 crew (ISS020-E-047807) Thunderstorms over the Amazon Basin along the Rio Madeira, seen from the International Space Station, 6 October 2009 (ISS020-E-047807). Photo: NASA/Johnson Space Center (Expedition 20 crew). Public domain. Cropped and resized. Source · Licence BushCreekEastFireAdamsLake Burned forest after the August 2023 blowup of the Bush Creek East wildfire, Adams Lake, British Columbia, 17 September 2023. Photo: The Interior, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Cropped and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence Menembus Kabut Asap \"Menembus Kabut Asap\" (Through the smoke haze): a monkey leaping through forest fire haze, South Kalimantan, Indonesia, 1 October 2023. Photo: Imam4rake, Wiki Loves Earth 2024, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Cropped and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence Smoky sun due to wildfire (54112316445) Sun dimmed by forest fire smoke, northern Pantanal near Poconé, Mato Grosso, Brazil, September 2024. Photo: Brian Ralphs (Flickr), via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY 2.0. Cropped and resized. Source · Licence Lago Tefé Lake Tefé, Amazonas, Brazil, as the first rains return after a long dry period, 9 October 2024. Photo: Gpgeo, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Cropped and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence Reserva de Desenvolvimento Sustentável Mamirauá - Adriana Brandao (02) Late afternoon in the Mamirauá Sustainable Development Reserve, Tefé, Amazonas, Brazil, with clouds announcing rain, 21 June 2025. Photo: Adriana Brandao (Adrisub), Wiki Loves Earth 2025, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Cropped and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence NASA's Orbiting Carbon Observatory (OCO)-2 (Artist Concept) (PIA18374) Artist's concept of NASA's Orbiting Carbon Observatory-2 (OCO-2), PIA18374, 2014. Image: Courtesy NASA/JPL-Caltech. Used under the JPL Image Use Policy. Cropped and resized. Source · Licence 2022 - Visita a Torre Atto - 51870028668 The Amazon Tall Tower Observatory (ATTO) above the rainforest near Manaus, Brazil, 4 February 2022. Photo: Wesley Sousa (ASCOM/SEAPC/MCTI), Flickr SintonizeMCTI, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY 2.0. Cropped and resized. Source · Licence Río Yukón, Carmacks, Yukón, Canadá, 2017-08-27, DD 01-04 PAN The Yukon River near Carmacks, Yukon, Canada, 27 August 2017. Photo: Diego Delso, delso.photo, License CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. Cropped from a panorama and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence Table iceberg west of Sjuøyane, Arctic ocean Table iceberg west of Sjuøyane, Svalbard, Arctic Ocean, 31 July 2015. Photo: Andreas Weith, via Wikimedia Commons, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Cropped and resized; this adapted version is also licensed CC BY-SA 4.0. Source · Licence Logos The logos of partner organisations are shown with a link to each organisation and remain the property of their owners."}];